The selected combination of processing parameters influences the overall quality of the fabricated part. The laser-beam power and velocity control the size and shape of the molten pool as the laser beam moves across the powder bed, while the hatch spacing determines the degree of remelting that occurs across a single layer. The selection of power, velocity, and hatch spacing will, in turn, dictate the selection of an appropriate layer height 19  and this interaction is illustrated schematically in  Figure   1 . For a fixed melt pool width and depth, the choice of a larger hatch spacing will necessitate the selection of a smaller layer height and vice versa, otherwise insufficient overlap between the adjacent melt pool will potentially lead to the formation of voids or lack-of-fusion defects. The parameters power ( q ), velocity ( v ), hatch spacing ( h ), and layer height ( l ) are sometimes grouped together in the following equation to form the term “volumetric energy density” ( E ): 17 , 21 1 E = q / v . l . h . \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
